                    },
                    "comment": "t.real - the real part of a complex tensor"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "text_block",
                    "id": 311,
                    "position_x": 862,
                    "position_y": 45,
                    "width": 768,
                    "height": 676,
                    "visibility": "show_all",
                    "draggable": true,
                    "presentation_state": "show_all",
                    "properties": {
                        "0": {
                            "name": "block",
                            "value": "Takes the real part out of a complex tensor, as an ordinary real tensor of the same shape.\n\nComplex tensors come out of t.fft and t.rfft, and most of what you want to do next needs real numbers: plotting, thresholding, feeding anything that is not complex-aware. t.real and t.imag split the two halves;\nt.complex puts them back together.\n\nTHE REAL PART IS NOT THE SIZE:\nA complex value's size (its magnitude, what abs gives) is the length of the two parts taken together. The real part alone depends on the phase: a component can be large and still have a real part near zero, if its angle happens to sit near a quarter turn. For \"how much of this frequency is there\", use abs on the complex tensor, not t.real. The demo shows the difference: a value of constant size whose real part swings.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.real\nIt takes no arguments.\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nThe complex tensor. A tensor that is not complex - including an ordinary float tensor, a list or a number - sends NOTHING, silently, rather than an answer of the value itself.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nreal tensor:\nThe real parts, as float32 from complex64 (float64 from complex128), the same shape as the input.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nmake_turning sends one complex number of size 1, going round a circle once every 4 seconds (the list shows it). The plot draws two lines: abs, flat at 1, and the real part from t.real, swinging as a cosine between -1 and 1. The size never changed; only the angle did.\n\nRELATED:\nt.imag takes the other half. t.complex builds a complex tensor from the two parts. abs gives the size. t.fft and t.rfft send complex tensors.",
                            "value_type": "str"
                        },
                        "1": {
                            "name": "lock",
                            "value": true,
                            "value_type": "bool"
                        },
                        "2": {
                            "name": "width",
                            "value": 760,
                            "value_type": "int"
                        },
                        "3": {
                            "name": "height",
                            "value": 660,
                            "value_type": "int"
                        },
                        "4": {
                            "name": "text_size",
                            "value": "30",
                            "value_type": "str"
                        }
                    },
                    "text": "Takes the real part out of a complex tensor, as an ordinary real tensor of the same shape.\n\nComplex tensors come out of t.fft and t.rfft, and most of what you want to do next needs real numbers: plotting, thresholding, feeding anything that is not complex-aware. t.real and t.imag split the two halves;\nt.complex puts them back together.\n\nTHE REAL PART IS NOT THE SIZE:\nA complex value's size (its magnitude, what abs gives) is the length of the two parts taken together. The real part alone depends on the phase: a component can be large and still have a real part near zero, if its angle happens to sit near a quarter turn. For \"how much of this frequency is there\", use abs on the complex tensor, not t.real. The demo shows the difference: a value of constant size whose real part swings.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.real\nIt takes no arguments.\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nThe complex tensor. A tensor that is not complex - including an ordinary float tensor, a list or a number - sends NOTHING, silently, rather than an answer of the value itself.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nreal tensor:\nThe real parts, as float32 from complex64 (float64 from complex128), the same shape as the input.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nmake_turning sends one complex number of size 1, going round a circle once every 4 seconds (the list shows it). The plot draws two lines: abs, flat at 1, and the real part from t.real, swinging as a cosine between -1 and 1. The size never changed; only the angle did.\n\nRELATED:\nt.imag takes the other half. t.complex builds a complex tensor from the two parts. abs gives the size. t.fft and t.rfft send complex tensors."
                },
